<h2>The background</h2>
<p>FIR Case Crime No. 04 of 2019, P.S. Ganj, District Rampur, alleged a criminal conspiracy under Section 120B IPC, with Sections 420, 467, 468 and 471, over two conflicting birth certificates issued in the name of Mohammad Abdullah Azam Khan — one from Rampur, one from Lucknow — and their alleged fraudulent use. The trial court convicted on 18 October 2023, and the appellate court affirmed on 23 December 2023. In revision, the Allahabad High Court, by a common judgment of 24 May 2024, suspended the sentences of all three convicts under Section 389(1) read with Sections 397/401 CrPC — but stayed only Mohammad Azam Khan's conviction, declining the same relief to his son and wife.</p>
<p>Three appeals reached the Supreme Court: the State challenged the stay granted to Azam Khan; Abdullah Azam Khan and Dr. Tazeen Fatma challenged the refusal of a stay to them.</p>

<h2>The State's procedural objections failed</h2>
<p>The State argued that Azam Khan's revision had sought only suspension of sentence, making the stay of conviction an order beyond the prayers, and that the High Court recorded no separate reasons for it. The Court rejected both points: the connected applications did seek interim relief, and the grounds the High Court gave for suspending sentence — time already served, age and ill health, no flight risk, and the absence of direct evidence that Azam Khan personally forged the municipal registers — were valid grounds for the stay as well.</p>

<h2>Two lines drawn</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>No stay for political calendars.</strong> The Court "unequivocally disavowed" stays of conviction sought on political expediency or electoral ambition, holding that courts cannot permit statutory powers to be leveraged to suit political calendars or electoral ambitions.</div>
  <div class="arrow">·</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Parity must be honoured or explained.</strong> Co-convicts on the same footing must receive the same treatment unless the order states an intelligible differentia. A silent departure is arbitrary under Article 14.</div>
</div>
<p>On the Article 21 side, the Court recognised that a conviction inflicts immediate stigma and reputational harm that cannot be undone if the conviction is later set aside, and that judicial discretion must balance those irreversible consequences against the sanctity of a conviction until it is displaced. The Court quoted at length from <em>Afzal Ansari v. State of U.P.</em>, (2024) 2 SCC 187, which in turn draws on <em>Ravikant S. Patil v. Sarvabhouma S. Bagali</em>, (2007) 1 SCC 673.</p>

<h2>The outcome, party by party</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Party</th><th>Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td>State of U.P.</td><td>Appeal dismissed — the stay of Azam Khan's conviction stands; no ground for interference under Article 136</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mohammad Azam Khan</td><td>Stay of conviction granted by the High Court upheld</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Abdullah Azam Khan</td><td>Appeal allowed — refusal of stay set aside as arbitrary; conviction stayed pending revision</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dr. Tazeen Fatma</td><td>Appeal allowed — same relief on parity</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The Court directed the High Court to decide the pending criminal revisions on their individual merits, uninfluenced by its observations, as expeditiously as possible.</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<p>For revision and appeal practice, three points stand out. First, a stay of conviction remains exceptional — counsel must plead the concrete, irreversible consequence the conviction is causing, not the election it is obstructing. Second, where interim relief has been granted to a similarly placed co-convict, parity is a constitutional argument, not a courtesy; conversely, a court refusing parity must articulate the differentia. Third, reasons given for suspending a sentence can, where they genuinely overlap, sustain a stay of conviction too — the absence of a separately labelled paragraph is not fatal.</p>
<div class="note">The judgment is non-reportable and turns on its facts, but its restatement of the parity principle and its rejection of electoral urgency as a ground will be cited in stay-of-conviction applications under Section 430 BNSS across trial and appellate forums.</div>
